1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,197
Is a bank statement enough proof

2
00:00:02,197 --> 00:00:03,499
for a business expense?

3
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:05,510
On its own? Usually not.

4
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:06,915
Look at this line.

5
00:00:06,915 --> 00:00:09,094
Corner Hardware, $62.

6
00:00:09,393 --> 00:00:11,040
Deck screws for a client job?

7
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:11,965
Or a birthday gift?

8
00:00:11,965 --> 00:00:13,678
The statement can’t tell you.

9
00:00:13,978 --> 00:00:15,450
The itemized receipt can.

10
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:17,193
Screws, glue, sandpaper.

11
00:00:17,643 --> 00:00:19,746
IRS guidance says proof that you paid

12
00:00:19,746 --> 00:00:21,510
doesn’t, on its own,

13
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:23,409
show you’re entitled to the deduction.

14
00:00:23,859 --> 00:00:24,775
So keep both.

15
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:26,460
The statement shows you paid.

16
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:28,172
The receipt shows what you bought.

17
00:00:28,672 --> 00:00:30,625
Find us at receipt-nest.com,

18
00:00:30,625 --> 00:00:32,371
on the App Store and Google Play.
